<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Transcribed by Divyash Maheshwari -->
<worksheet xml:id="se1e">
  <title>Linear Algebra Sample Exam 1E</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      <em>Work done on scratch paper will not be graded. You don't need to show your work on pages 1 and 2.</em>
    </p>
  </introduction>
<!-- Page 1 -->
    <page>
    <exercise workspace="2in">
        <statement>
        <p>
            (7 points) Indicate <term>true</term> if the statement is true, otherwise, indicate <term>false</term>.
        </p>
        <tabular halign="left">
            <col width="88%" />
            <col width="6%" />
            <col width="6%" />
            <row bottom="minor" >
                <cell></cell>
                <cell>true</cell>
                <cell>false</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>a) If <m>\vec{c}_1</m> and <m>\vec{c}_2</m> are the standard vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>, and <m>T(\vec{x})= A\vec{x}</m> is a matrix transform that satisfies <m>T(\vec{c}_1) = T(\vec{c}_2) = 1</m>, then <m>A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; 1 \\ \begin{pmatrix}</m>.</cell>
                <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>b) If <m> A</m> is <m>2 \times 3</m>, then the transformation <m>\vec{x} \to A\vec{x}</m> cannot be onto.</cell>
                <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>c) If <m>\vec{x}_1</m> is a solution to the inhomogeneous system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>, then any vector in Span<m>\{\vec{x}_1}</m> is also a solution to <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>.</cell>
                <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>d) If there are non-trivial solutions to the linear system <m>A\{x} = \vec{0}</m>, then the columns of <m>A</m> must be linearly independent.</cell>
                <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>e) If the linear system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> is inconsistent, then <m>\vec{b}</m> cannot be in the span of the columns of <m>A</m>.</cell>
                <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>f) If <m>\vec{x}</m> is a nontrivial solution of <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m>, then every entry of <m>\vec{x}</m> is nonzero.</cell>
                <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>g) Suppose <m>A</m> and <m>E</m> are <m>n \times n</m> matrices. If <m>A</m> is row equivalent to <m>E</m>, and the first column of <m>A</m> is pivotal, then the first column of <m>E</m> is also pivotal.</cell>
                <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell></cell>
            </row>
        </tabular>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                (5 points) Indicate whether the situations are <term>possible</term> or <term>impossible</term>.
            </p>
            <tabular>
                <col width="76%" />
                <col width="12%" />
                <col width="12%" />
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell>possible</cell>
                    <cell>impossible</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>a) <m>A</m> is a <m>5 \times </m> matrix that has 3<m>3</m> pivotal columns, and <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> is a linear system with <m>2</m> free variables.</cell>
                    <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>b) <m>A</m> is a <m>3 \times 4</m> matrix whose columns are linearly independent and span <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>.</cell>
                    <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>c) The augmented matrix <m>\left( \begin{array}{c|c} A &amp; b \\ \end{array} \right)</m> does not have a pivot in the last column and represents an inconsistent linear system.</cell>
                    <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>d) <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> is a one-to-one linear transform, <m>A</m> is <m>m \times n</m>, and <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> is inconsistent for some <m>\vec{b} \in \mathbb{R}^m</m>.</cell>
                    <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>e) <m>T(\vec{x})</m> is a linear transform that maps vectors in <m>R^n</m> to <m>\mathbb{R}</m>, </cell>
                    <cell><m>\bigcirc</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                    <cell></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
    </page>

</worksheet>